---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Heading from "../components/Heading.astro";
import { AUTHOR, SPONSOR, GITHUB_ORG, NPM_ORG, PACKAGES, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_MOTTO } from "../consts";
import pkg from "../../package.json";

// What the project is, who runs it, and how it is paid for — in one place.
//
// The material is spread across /why (the argument), /community (how to take
// part) and /sponsors (who funds it), which is right for a reader following a
// thread and wrong for anyone — person or program — arriving with the plain
// question "what is this and who is behind it". This page answers that and
// links onward rather than restating those pages.
//
// The counts and versions are derived, never typed, for the same reason they
// are derived everywhere else on this site.
const engine = (pkg.dependencies as Record<string, string>)["@tabnas/parser"];
const packageCount = PACKAGES.length;
const grammarCount = PACKAGES.filter((p) => p.tier === "grammar").length;
const goCount = PACKAGES.filter((p) => p.go).length;
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What tabnas is, who builds it, how it is funded, and what state it is in — the tabnas project in one page, with the links to check any of it."
>
  <article class="about" data-pagefind-body>
    <Heading level={1} id="about-tabnas">About tabnas</Heading>

    <p class="lede">
      <i>tabnas</i> is an open source parsing engine. It is a project, not a
      product: nothing here is sold, there is no account to create, and the
      code runs on your machine rather than ours.
    </p>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="what-it-is">What it is</Heading>
      <p>
        A parser normally arrives as generated code, or as recursive descent
        written by hand. Either way, extending someone else's language means
        forking their parser and owning the fork. <i>tabnas</i> takes the other
        route: the engine is a for-loop over tokens with a rule stack, and a
        grammar is the lookup table it walks. Because that table is ordinary
        data, extending a language means adding rows to a table that already
        works — JSON becomes JSONC becomes jsonic becomes YAML, each one a
        plugin over the last.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second consequence is why the project has an
        <a href="/agents">agent-facing half</a>. A flat, declarative, control-flow-free
        table is a good compile target: RFC 5234 ABNF compiles into it, and so
        does the output of a language model — and unlike generated code, you
        can validate the result before you run it.
        <a href="/why">Why tabnas</a> is the long-form version of this
        argument, and the source document for everything else on the site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="what-ships">What ships</Heading>
      <p>
        {packageCount} packages: the engine, the grammar tooling,
        {" "}{grammarCount} language and data-format grammars, the syntax
        plugins, and the agent distributions — Agent Skills, an
        <a href="/mcp">MCP server</a> and the <code>tabnas</code> command-line
        tool. {goCount} of them ship as a Go module as well as an npm package,
        from one shared set of fixtures that both runtimes must pass.
        <a href="/releases">Releases</a> lists every one with its current
        version; <a href="/versions.json"><code>/versions.json</code></a> is
        the same answer for a program.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is MIT licensed. The source is on GitHub under
        {" "}<a href={GITHUB_ORG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/tabnas</a>,
        and the packages are published to npm under
        {" "}<a href={NPM_ORG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">the @tabnas scope</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="who">Who builds it</Heading>
      <p>
        <i>tabnas</i> is written by
        {" "}<a href={AUTHOR.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{AUTHOR.name}</a>,
        who has been building parsers and open source Node.js libraries for a
        long time — the engine grew out of
        {" "}<a href="https://github.com/jsonicjs/jsonic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jsonic</a>,
        a forgiving JSON parser, which is still one of the grammars here.
      </p>
      <p>
        One maintainer is a real constraint and the site says so rather than
        implying a team. Contributions arrive through GitHub and are welcome,
        including ones written with an AI agent —
        <a href="/community">community</a> explains how that works and why the
        shared fixtures make it safe.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="funding">How it is funded</Heading>
      <p>
        {SPONSOR.blurb} There is no commercial edition, no hosted plan to
        upgrade to, and no telemetry in any package. The one service the
        project operates is the hosted MCP endpoint, which exists for agent
        clients that cannot spawn a local process; it stores nothing, and the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> is short because there is
        nothing to describe. <a href="/sponsors">Sponsors</a> has the detail.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="state">What state it is in</Heading>
      <p>
        Pre-1.0, and pinned exactly for that reason: the engine is at
        {" "}<code>{engine}</code>, and a caret range would silently accept the
        next minor. The parser is deterministic, which means it does not
        handle ambiguous grammars, and you supply the lexer's token
        definitions.
        <a href="/comparisons">Other parsers</a> names the cases where ANTLR,
        Peggy, Chevrotain, nearley or tree-sitter is the better answer, and
        the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> has a scope section for the things this
        project will not do.
      </p>
      <p>
        Per-repository CI, release and compliance state is published at
        <a href="/status">status</a>, so the claim that both runtimes agree is
        one you can check rather than take.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="reach-us">Getting in touch</Heading>
      <p>
        Questions and bug reports go through GitHub;
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> reaches the
        maintainer directly. <a href="/contact">Contact</a> has the right
        channel for each kind of message.
      </p>
      <p class="motto">
        <i>{SITE_MOTTO}</i> — Irish for "a table of translations", which is
        what a grammar is.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about { max-width: 52rem; }
  .about :global(h1) { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 2.8rem); margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .about :global(h2) { font-size: 1.35rem; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 62ch; }
  .group { margin-top: 2.75rem; }
  .group p { color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 68ch; }
  .motto { font-family: var(--font-heading); color: var(--ink-mute); }
</style>
